t to broaden the tax base  in order to manage what is going to be a  very difficult and bumpy period  economically and the real challenge from  the chancellor which we can see coming  through the telegraph  is that politically and economically the  two cycles you know don't quite match up  ideally the chancellor would want to be  more cautious in terms of balancing the  books right now  so as not to choke off a recovery but  politically  if you wait too late you're too close to  an election to really increase taxes  and that is a fine line the chancellor  is going to be trying to walk  next week yeah he's damned if he does he  damned if he doesn't rachel isn't he and  of course the conservatives promised no  tax rises didn't they i mean do you  think people would understand  given everything that's happened over  the last 12 months if uh if he has to  bend the rules somewhat or bend that  promise  it's an interesting one isn't it because  the public has always consistently  been in favor of higher tax for the  highest earners  and favored favored hikes incorporation  tax as well  and we can see that reiterated now in  the most recent polling  shows that now in the midst of a  pandemic of course  support for um those tax measures  is still there if not more so and i  think that  when we look at uh this budget this week  a lot a lot of it will be the focus for  a lot of it i think will be on  how where is the tax coming from who is  it that's being taxed  now it looks like the chancellor is  talking about  um an online tax so looking at companies  that have done so well  when so many of us have only been able  to shop online  but on the other hand it's it's looking  like he's backing away from a windfall  tax which just does look like a missed  opportunity  um we do not want a situation when the  people worst affected by the pandemic  are hit  even harder in the recovery from it  and a winfor tax is such a great way to  make sure that doesn't happen  it looks it looks at taxing companies  like amazon which amazon uk had 50  increase in sales in the pandemic year  circo  circo a company that got billions um  in prior in contracts using public  sector money  for private contracts um made 36  uh increase in profits this year the  idea that you wouldn't put  a win for tax on companies like that  does seem bizarre  uh anita front page of the observer  again soonax 5 billion pound  rescue plan to stop high streets from  collapse i mean the big fear of course  is uh  employment and uh the issues that we're  going to face  uh this year with millions of people  struggling uh to keep their jobs  the chancellor wanting to offer seven  hundred thousand shops pubs restaurants  etc  these grants and of course it's young  people isn't it who work in the  hospitality sector  who are most likely to suffer i mean do  you do you think this  will go far enough this 5 billion pounds  um i think the chancellor is doing  absolutely everything possible to keep  as many jobs as possible but ultimately  we are in a situation where there is a  huge  unknown which is how quickly demand gets  accelerated through the economy again  once you know the magic date the 21st of  june  arrives and it's really not clear at  this point and we still have the ongoing  threat of possible new strains of the  virus that will hinder  and slow down the reopening of the  economy we're just really not sure  whether households are going to take a  very cautious approach to spending again  when it comes to the summer and knowing  and remembering the the pain of the past  year  or whether they are going to want to  just get out there and spend some of the  income that some families have built up  over this period  so there are so many unknowns in this  and ultimately it's really  whether these um hospitality businesses  end up being viable it's not really  going to be down to  whether they get support from the  chancellor it's really going to be about  whether there is a genuine demand in the  economy  for those services and that really  probably all comes down to  whether and how social distancing  continues through the summer  yeah you make a very good point there uh  look at the front page of the sunday  time stealth rise in income tax to pay  kovid  bill i mean that's the thing rachel  isn't it i mean the bank of england's  chief economist said recently  that we're going to be like a coiled  spring  um that there's a lot of sort of pent up  financial energy and there needs to be  doesn't there in order for us all to get  out  and spend again to keep the economy  going do you think it'll be there  it depends which bit of the picture  you're looking at i think it's  definitely true that there are a lot of  people who during the pandemic have had  an opportunity to  to to save i think they were dubbed um  the sourdough savers by some economists  just because you know people with  disposable  income not having anywhere to dispose it  on the other hand  a lot of people have been really  struggling and really hit in this  pandemic  um household debt has risen during this  pandemic  so again what we're looking at is a very  real risk  that the rampant inequalities we have in  this country  will deepen even further if the  chancellor does not  manage the economy with a budget that is  conscious of that and unfortunately i  don't see any sign  or any awareness of that with the things  that being trailed so far  so when we look at needing to stimulate  the economy i think that is absolutely  true  but some of the ways that you stimulate  the economy is to invest  you invest in skills you invest in  training you might want to look at  raising the living wage  so that people have more money to spend  in the economy you might especially want  to look at doing that for people who  have borne the brunt of the pandemic  carried all the risk in the pandemic so  that we are able to come through it so  we're looking at frontline workers  not just nhs but other frontline workers  they're the sort of people who um  i think deserve a pay raise and it would  be good to see that happen  in this week's budget anita you've been  shaking your head do you want to come  back  well i just think this is just not  reflecting the reality that we've seen  from the chancellor which is that the  inferno scheme has kept a lot of people  particularly in hospitality going  and that is what we're going to probably  see extended through the budget so i  think the chancellor is very conscious  of those people that have been  struggling the most  and secondly on this point about the  national living wage  we've just talked about how much of the  hospitality industry  might potentially go to the wall and how  much they need an emergency injection of  cash  if we combine that with an increase in  the in the national living wage  that might be great for people that  still have their jobs but there's going  to be an awful lot of people  and an awful lot of businesses who will  not be able  to to weather an increase in salaries so  i think the chancellor is taking a very  cautious approach  but we'll see and i'm expecting to see  an uplift in uc for instance which i  think  is really speaking to that lower income  group of people who have really  struggled  over the past year okay i'm going to  have to leave it there i'm afraid we've  got to go to the break  thank you both full coverage of course  of the budget here on sky news on  wednesday  coming up doctors are warning of a  mental health crisis among children  and young people caused by the pandemic  discussing that and more  in a couple of minutes come back time  for part two of the press preview  rachel and anita still with me uh front  page of the  observer rachel i mean children have  really suffered haven't they as we know  over the last 12 months the pandemic  really has been extremely difficult for  for youngsters and doctors now fear a  new  child mental health crisis  sadly yes i 